course: dictionary
lesson: 6204
title: A missile is a thing sent at a target
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A missile is something that flies through the air towards a target. «Do not throw stones or other missiles» is a school safety rule. A stone, a ball or a bottle thrown at someone is a missile too, because it can hurt them. Ask yourself: what is flying at a target?
explain: Missile can also mean a powerful weapon, often a rocket that carries explosives. In news about war you may read «missile attack». Words that go with missile are rocket, launch and target: a rocket is launched, then it flies at its target.
explain: Try swapping your meaning into the sentence. «Do not throw stones or other rockets» sounds odd, because a stone is not a rocket. «Do not throw stones or other missiles» makes sense, because stones are thrown objects. If your meaning fits, you are probably right.
fact: Missile means a weapon that flies through the air towards a target.
fact: A missile is often powered by a rocket and may carry explosives.
fact: Any object thrown at someone or something can also be called a missile, such as a stone or a bottle.
fact: «The peregrine falcon is a missile» compares a fast, straight-flying bird to a missile, showing how quickly and directly it flies at its target.
fact: Related words: rocket, launch, target.
exercise: «Do not throw stones or other missiles.» In this sentence, what is a missile? | A soft pillow for sleeping | A tall tree in the park | A kind of fish that lives in rivers | *A thing thrown that could hurt someone
exercise: «The peregrine falcon is a missile.» Why might an author call a falcon a missile? | It is a sleepy bird that rests all day | *It flies very fast and straight at its target | It is slow and heavy like a rock | It is made of metal and carries explosives
exercise: Find and copy the word that means the place a missile is aimed at. | sirens | *target | basement | shelter
test: A missile is best described as | A sweet that melts in your mouth | A bird that sings in trees | A kind of soft blanket | *A weapon, or any object thrown, that flies at a target
test: Which sentence uses missile to mean a thrown object? | I like painting pictures | *Do not throw stones or other missiles | The bus stopped at the corner | Please pass the salt
exam: Ella says a missile can only be a rocket. Is she right? | Yes, every missile is a rocket | *No, a stone or bottle thrown at someone can also be a missile | No, because missiles are only ever birds | Yes, because missiles are always made of metal
